2026-09-27T09:27:24.8153095Z Disclaimer: there's no guarantee of timely updates in case of security problems or other issues. If you want to use them in a security-or-otherwise-critical environment (say, on a production server), you do so at your own risk.
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8154944Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8155127Z Update Note
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8155592Z ===========
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8156273Z Please use this repository instead of ppa:fkrull/deadsnakes.
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8156859Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8157042Z Reporting Issues
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8157484Z ================
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8157730Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8158055Z Issues can be reported in the master issue tracker at:
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8158775Z https://github.com/deadsnakes/issues/issues
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8159237Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8159472Z Supported Ubuntu and Python Versions
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8160012Z ====================================
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8160362Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8160972Z - Ubuntu 22.04 (jammy) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.10)
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8161666Z - Ubuntu 24.04 (noble) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.12)
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8162278Z - Ubuntu 26.04 (resolute) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.14)
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8163501Z - Note: Python 3.10 (jammy), Python3.12 (noble), Python3.14 (resolute) are not provided by deadsnakes as upstream ubuntu provides those packages.
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8164523Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8164726Z Why some packages aren't built:
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8165528Z - Note: for 22.04+, older python versions requre libssl<3 so they are not currently built
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8166530Z - If you need these, reach out to asottile to set up a private ppa
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8167053Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8167586Z The packages may also work on other versions of Ubuntu or Debian, but that is not tested or supported.
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8168286Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8168449Z Packages
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8168767Z ========
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8168953Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8173031Z The packages provided here are loosely based on the debian upstream packages with some modifications to make them more usable as non-default pythons and on ubuntu.  As such, the packages follow debian's patterns and often do not include a full python distribution with just `apt install python#.#`.  Here is a list of packages that may be useful along with the default install:
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8175511Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8175904Z - `python#.#-dev`: includes development headers for building C extensions
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8244154Z - `python#.#-venv`: provides the standard library `venv` module
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8245611Z - `python#.#-distutils`: provides the standard library `distutils` module
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8246784Z - `python#.#-lib2to3`: provides the `2to3-#.#` utility as well as the standard library `lib2to3` module
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8247635Z - `python#.#-gdbm`: provides the standard library `dbm.gnu` module
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8248356Z - `python#.#-tk`: provides the standard library `tkinter` module
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8249062Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8249227Z Third-Party Python Modules
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8249606Z ==========================
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8249845Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8251782Z Python modules in the official Ubuntu repositories are packaged to work with the Python interpreters from the official repositories. Accordingly, they generally won't work with the Python interpreters from this PPA. As an exception, pure-Python modules for Python 3 will work, but any compiled extension modules won't.
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8253760Z 
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8254887Z To install 3rd-party Python modules, you should use the common Python packaging tools.  For an introduction into the Python packaging ecosystem and its tools, refer to the Python Packaging User Guide:
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8256427Z https://packaging.python.org/installing/
2026-09-27T09:27:24.8256803Z 
